On Jul 14, 4:17 pm, syullf....@gmail.com wrote:

> I happen to have a problem Y that I would like fixed: to me, infinite
> generation feels inelegant. One resource, a pudding, should not be
> able to generate an infinite number of items.

I think this is closer to the actual problem, or at least one of them.
Remember that wandering monsters, and their drops, come straight from
D&D of the 1970s, which was the original inspiration of the game
Rogue. (D&D = 1970s. AD&D = c.1980s+.)  Wandering monsters, which are
presumptively in infinite supply (to a point, this is reasonable),
provide a random entertainment (and danger) element in the game, and
the drops are basically what you would expect a monster to be carrying
around with it.  If you clobber a guy in the street, you will probably
expect him to have a wallet when you get around to probing his
motionless body. If you clobber a guy in the street, and he summons
another guy, or "divides" into two of him, would you expect to have
two wallets? Yes, I think so. If he "cloned" himself, you would expect
the two drops to be exactly the same. But what if you clobber a
pudding in the street, and it divides? Will there be more stuff?
Conceptually, I think "no." Will the second pudding still fight like
the first? I think "yes." It just won't be carrying more stuff pulled
from thin air.

If you look at it from that perspective, I think that possible
solutions emerge, the most obvious of which is that when a monster is
created by physical division, the objects it is carrying are created
by dividing up ones carried by the parent. Exceptions could logically
be made, though, in the case of food drops (perhaps every black
pudding has a yummy nougat center) and so on. If it's an Andromeda
Strain type creature that has gems as part of its physical makeup,
then of course you'd expect gems in each divided creature's drop.

Meanwhile, farming puddings for practice, that makes *perfect* sense
to me. You go to the firing range for pistol practice and to the
driving range for golf practice, and both make you more skilled. I see
no reason why pudding practice shouldn't make you a more skilled, more
powerful character.



